.TH HOGAR-SHELL 5 "" "hogar-shell 0.1.0" "hogar-shell"
.SH NAME
hogar-shell \- configuration file format
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I ~/.config/hogar-shell/config.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
hogar-shell is configured in TOML. Every key below is optional; the default shown is what the shell uses
when the key is absent, so a working config is any subset of this page.
.PP
The file is watched and re\-read on save, and the change applies without a restart. A file under
.I ~/.config/hogar-shell/monitors/<output>/
has the same shape and overrides the global one for that monitor.
.PP
.B hogar-shell config schema
prints this same reference as a complete, valid config file, and
.B hogar-shell config schema <section>
prints one section of it. Both come from the running build rather than from this page.
.SH SECTIONS
.SS [active_window]
The `active_window` module. `compact` shows the app's class instead of the document title \(em stable while you
move around inside one app, and much narrower.
.sp
Nothing bounds the title's length. The chip gives up width when its side of the bar runs short and the label
elides, so a browser tab that runs to a paragraph costs the modules beside it nothing. A character count
bounded it once and could not tell the two cases apart: it cut a short title on a wide bar exactly as
readily as a long one on a narrow bar, which is the wrong unit for a question about room.
.sp
`inverted` puts the icon after the title instead of before it. Which reads better depends on where the chip
sits: leading the icon points into the bar from the left, and trailing it does the same from the right, so
a chip in the end zone usually wants this on.
.TP
.B compact
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.TP
.B inverted
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.TP
.B show_icon
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.SS [animation]
How the shell moves (`[animation]`).
.sp
Two curve families rather than one, because rsx has two motion models and they answer different questions.
`curve` names a **spring**, for motion that chases a target that can move mid\-flight \(em the workspace
indicator, which has to bend its path when you hold a workspace key rather than restart. `easing` names a
**timing function**, for a transition with a start, an end and a duration \(em a panel opening.
.sp
`duration_scale` multiplies every duration at once, so "make it all a bit quicker" is one number; `enabled
= false` collapses every duration to zero, which is the accessibility answer (and what a user on a remote
desktop wants) rather than a per\-surface opt\-out.
.TP
.B curve
The spring preset for continuous motion: `gentle`, `snappy` or `bouncy`.
.sp
Default: \fB"gentle"\fR
.TP
.B duration_scale
Default: \fB1.0\fR
.TP
.B easing
The timing function for duration\-based transitions: `linear`, `ease\-in`, `ease\-out`, `ease\-in\-out`.
.sp
Default: \fB"ease\-out"\fR
.TP
.B enabled
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B panel_duration_ms
How long a panel takes to enter or leave, before `duration_scale`.
.sp
Default: \fB180\fR
.SS [audio]
Audio control (`[audio]`). `increment` is what one wheel notch over the volume or microphone chip moves and
what `hogar\-shell volume up` steps by. `max_volume` is the ceiling the sink can be raised to: PipeWire lets a
sink boost past 100 %, which rescues a quiet laptop and wrecks a good speaker, so it belongs to the user
rather than to a constant in the code.
.TP
.B increment
Default: \fB5\fR
.TP
.B max_volume
Default: \fB150\fR
.SS [background]
Full\-screen wallpaper behind everything, one surface per monitor. Off by default so the compositor's own background shows through; setting an `image` \(em or `enabled = true` for a plain themed background \(em turns it on. `[background.monitors]` maps output names to per\-monitor images, each falling back to the global `image`, and `hogar\-shell wallpaper set` overrides both at runtime. Paths may use `~`.
.TP
.B enabled
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.TP
.B transition
How a change from one wallpaper to the next is drawn: `fade` (the default), `wipe` or `none`.
.sp
Default: \fB"fade"\fR
.TP
.B transition_ms
How long that transition runs, before `[animation] duration_scale`. Ignored while `[animation] enabled` is off, which makes every change instant.
.sp
Default: \fB600\fR
.TP
.B image
Unset by default.
.SS [background.monitors]
.SS [bars]
One bar per screen edge; empty bars collapse to zero. Default is all\-empty by design (serde fills missing fields), so configs get only what they specify \(em see [`Config::starter`] for the initial setup.
.sp
`excluded_screens` names outputs that get no bars at all \(em a TV, a projector, a monitor that only ever shows
one fullscreen thing. Each entry matches the connector name (`DP\-1`) as a `*` pattern, so `HDMI\-*` covers a
port whose index moves between reboots. *Which* modules a screen shows is a per\-monitor config override
(`monitors/<output>/config.toml`) rather than a key here: it is the same `[bars.<edge>]` shape, so there is
nothing new to learn and nothing to keep in step.
.TP
.B excluded_screens
Default: \fB[]\fR
.SS [bars.bottom]
.TP
.B center
Default: \fB[]\fR
.TP
.B end
Default: \fB[]\fR
.TP
.B peek
How many logical pixels of a hidden bar stay on screen, as the strip that reveals it. Too thin to hit
is worse than absent, so this is floored at 1.
.sp
Default: \fB2\fR
.TP
.B persistent
Keep the bar on screen at all times (the default). Switched off, it reserves no space and sits off its
own edge with only `peek` pixels showing, sliding in when the pointer reaches that strip and back out
when the pointer leaves.
.sp
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B show_on_hover
Reveal a non\-persistent bar when the pointer reaches its peek strip. Switched off, only a drag inward
past `[panels] drag_threshold` brings it in \(em which is what a touch screen wants, and what a pointer
that keeps brushing the screen edge on its way somewhere else does not.
.sp
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B size
Default: \fB34\fR
.TP
.B start
Default: \fB[]\fR
.SS [bars.bottom.shape]
.TP
.B mode
Unset by default.
.TP
.B gap
Unset by default.
.TP
.B spacing
Unset by default.
.TP
.B radius
Unset by default.
