Default: \fB70.0\fR
.SS [theme]
Theme selection and overrides. `name` picks a built\-in palette, `custom`, or `dynamic` (a palette generated from the current wallpaper); the rest override individual tokens on top of it \(em numbers directly, `[theme.scale]` proportionally, and `[theme.colors]` per\-token hex (`base = "#2e3440"`), keyed by the same names [`NordTheme::accent_by_name`] uses. Any unset field keeps the built\-in's value.
.TP
.B accent
Default: \fB"cyan"\fR
.TP
.B fallback
The palette a `dynamic` theme falls back to before a wallpaper has been quantised \(em on the very first
start, or with no wallpaper set at all.
.sp
Default: \fB"nord"\fR
.TP
.B mode
`dark`, `light`, or `auto` (the default) to keep whatever the named palette already is. A built\-in with
a sibling in the asked\-for mode switches to it (`gruvbox` \(<> `gruvbox\-light`); one without keeps its own.
.sp
Default: \fB"auto"\fR
.TP
.B name
Default: \fB"nord"\fR
.TP
.B opacity
How opaque every surface the shell paints is, from `0.2` to `1.0` \(em bars, panels, cards and flashes
alike. One key for the whole shell and no way to break it apart: a drawer at an opacity the bar it
hangs off does not share is not a look anybody chooses, it is two settings that drifted.
.sp
**This is the half a compositor cannot supply.** Blur behind a surface is the compositor's job \(em a
`layer_rule = blur, ^hogar\-shell`, which needs no code here \(em and it shows nothing through a surface
painted opaque. Lowering this is what gives it something to blur.
.sp
Default: \fB1.0\fR
.TP
.B variant
How much colour a `dynamic` scheme carries: `vibrant` (the default), `content`, `expressive`, `fidelity`
or `muted`. Ignored by the built\-in palettes, which carry their own.
.sp
Default: \fB"vibrant"\fR
